        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          For one to three mostly flat acres, the Toro TimeCutter 50 zero-turn is the mower to buy. With slopes or towing, choose the John Deere X380 lawn tractor, and for three to four acres, the Cub Cadet XT2 GX54 garden tractor or the EGO Z6 52 battery zero-turn.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="how-we-picked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How We Picked
          </h2>

          <p>
            A large yard can mean very different things. Half an acre with a slope, two flat acres full of trees and three acres of open grass each call for a different machine, and the most common mistake is buying the mower that suits someone else's lawn. So rather than ranking one type, we chose the best option for each common size and layout, from a wide walk-behind to a garden tractor and two battery machines.
          </p>

          <p>
            We built the list from manufacturer spec sheets, dealer listings and independent reference sites, and we did not run these mowers through a lab or timed course. Every engine, deck, battery and warranty figure on the product cards is credited to where we found it. Battery acreage ratings in particular are the maker's figures under good conditions, and we say so each time.
          </p>

          <p>
            We weighted deck width and build quality most heavily, because on a big lawn they decide both how long each mow takes and how long the mower lasts. After that we looked at how the mower handles your terrain, how it fits through gates and into storage, and how much time and money it costs to keep running.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you have already decided on a type, our dedicated roundups go deeper: the <a href="/best-zero-turn-mower/">best zero-turn mowers</a>, the <a href="/best-lawn-tractor/">best lawn tractors</a> and the <a href="/best-riding-lawn-mower/">best riding mowers</a> overall. This page is for owners still deciding which kind of mower their acreage needs.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="by-acreage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Which Mower Type Fits Your Acreage
          </h2>

          <p>
            Below half an acre, a quality 21-inch <a href="/best-self-propelled-lawn-mower/">self-propelled mower</a> is almost always enough. Between half an acre and an acre, a wide walk-behind like the 30-inch Toro TimeMaster or an entry-level riding mower both work, and the choice comes down to slopes, storage and how much walking you want to do.
          </p>

          <p>
            Between one and two acres, a riding mower becomes the sensible choice for most people. A 42- to 48-inch lawn tractor suits lawns with slopes or a need to tow, while a 42- to 50-inch zero-turn suits flat lawns with many trees and beds. Battery riders rated for two acres or more, like the Ryobi 46-inch, now cover this range too.
          </p>

          <p>
            Between two and four acres, deck width becomes the dominant factor. A 50- to 54-inch zero-turn or garden tractor cuts each pass about 20 to 30 percent wider than a 42-inch machine, which adds up to an hour or more saved every week across a season. At this size a fabricated deck and a stronger transaxle are worth paying for, because the mower is working hard every week.
          </p>

          <p>
            Above four acres you are into larger residential or light commercial zero-turns with 54- to 60-inch decks, or a compact tractor with a finishing mower if you also need loader or tillage work. Our <a href="/best-zero-turn-mower/">zero-turn roundup</a> includes bigger-deck machines for that range.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Lawn Size</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Best Type</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical Deck</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Our Pick</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">1/2 to 1 acre</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Wide walk-behind or small rider</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">30 to 42 in</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Toro TimeMaster 30</td>
                  </tr>
