        <div id="terrain" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Slopes, Obstacles and Open Ground
          </h2>

          <p>
            After acreage, your terrain decides the mower type. Zero-turns steer by driving each rear wheel independently and have caster front wheels that do not steer, so on a side slope they rely entirely on rear-wheel grip. That makes them fast and nimble on flat ground and less secure on hills. Lawn and garden tractors steer through the front wheels and keep better control on moderate slopes.
          </p>

          <p>
            Most manufacturers state a maximum slope in the operator's manual, and on riding mowers it is often around 15 degrees, though you should check the exact figure for your model. On anything steeper, a walk-behind is the safer tool. Our <a href="/best-lawn-mower-for-hills/">guide to mowers for hills</a> covers slope limits and safe technique in detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            Obstacles change the math in the other direction. A lawn with dozens of trees, beds and fence lines is where a zero-turn earns its price, because it pivots around each one instead of making three-point turns. On an open lawn with long straight runs, a lawn tractor loses little time and gains towing ability and slope security.
          </p>

          <p>
            Soft or wet ground favors lighter machines with wider tires. A heavy garden tractor can rut a soggy lawn in spring, and a zero-turn pivoting on wet turf can tear it. If your lawn stays wet, wider tires, slower turns and waiting a day after heavy rain help more than any particular mower type.
          </p>

          <p>
            Edges and trimming are part of the terrain question too. A wide rider leaves more strips along fences, beds and walls than a narrow walk-behind, so most large-yard owners also need a string trimmer to finish. If your lawn has long fence lines or many beds, budget the trimming time and the tool alongside the mower, because it can add a quarter of an hour or more to every mow.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="deck-width" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Deck Width Saves More Time Than Horsepower
          </h2>

          <p>
            On a large yard, the width of each pass drives your mowing time more than anything else. Going from a 42-inch deck to a 54-inch deck widens each pass by nearly 30 percent, and that saving repeats on every pass, every week. On three acres it can cut a two-and-a-half-hour mow by half an hour or more.
          </p>

          <p>
            Speed matters too, but less than it seems. A zero-turn rated at 7 or 8 mph will rarely mow at that speed, because cut quality falls apart and bumps become uncomfortable. In practice most owners mow somewhere well below top speed. Wider decks give you more coverage at a comfortable pace.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not buy the widest deck without checking access. Measure your narrowest gate and shed door, and remember the discharge chute adds width. A 54-inch deck that cannot reach the back lawn is worse than a 48-inch deck that can.
          </p>

          <p>
            Deck construction matters more as width grows. Wide stamped decks flex and rust, while fabricated decks, like the 10-gauge deck EGO lists on the Z6 52 or the 11-gauge deck Cub Cadet lists on the GX54, stand up to years of hard use. On a big lawn mown every week, a fabricated deck is worth the extra money.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cut quality also depends on deck width in a less obvious way. Wider decks use three blades instead of two, and the overlap between blades is where uneven cuts and stripes of missed grass appear if the deck is not level. Check the deck level each spring with the tires at the correct pressure, and adjust it following the manual. A level deck cuts evenly at any width, while an unlevel one scalps high spots and leaves ridges.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="gas-vs-battery" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Gas vs Battery on a Big Lawn
          </h2>

          <p>
            Battery riding mowers have changed what is possible on large lawns. Ryobi rates its 46-inch lawn tractor for up to 2.5 acres per charge and EGO rates its Z6 52-inch zero-turn for up to 4 acres with six batteries. Those are manufacturer figures under good conditions, but they put battery riders squarely into the large-yard range.
          </p>

          <p>
            The upside is real: no gas to store, no oil changes, no spark plugs or air filters, quiet operation and instant starts. Owners who mow early in the morning or have close neighbors notice the noise difference immediately. Maintenance falls to blades, belts and tires.
          </p>

          <p>
            The downside is cost and conditions. Battery riders cost more up front than comparable gas machines, and runtime drops in tall, thick or wet grass, so spring mowing and overgrown lawns will cut into the rated acreage. Battery packs also wear over the years and are expensive to replace, so check the battery warranty carefully.
          </p>

          <p>
            If your lawn is near the top of a battery mower's rated range, choose a gas model or a battery mower rated well above your acreage. If it sits comfortably within the rating, a battery rider is a pleasant way to mow. Our <a href="/best-battery-powered-lawn-mower/">battery mower roundup</a> covers smaller walk-behind options as well.
          </p>

          <p>
            Charging logistics matter on a big lawn as well. A battery rider needs a covered space with a suitable outlet, and some owners split a large mow across two days to avoid waiting for a recharge. Check the charge time and whether the batteries can be charged on board or must be removed, and consider whether an extra set of batteries is worth buying for your acreage.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="storage-cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Storage, Price and Running Costs
          </h2>

          <p>
            A large-yard mower needs a home. A 50- to 54-inch rider takes up most of a single garage bay once you allow space to walk around it and work on it, and it must be stored out of the rain to keep the deck from rusting and the seat and electronics from failing. A 30-inch walk-behind fits in a shed corner. Plan the storage before you buy.
          </p>

          <p>
            Purchase prices span a wide range. At the time of our research a retailer listed the Toro TimeCutter 50 at $4,599, Home Depot listed the Ryobi 46-inch at $5,499, and EGO's launch price for the Z6 52 with batteries was $6,999. Wide walk-behinds cost far less. Prices change often, so check current listings.
          </p>

          <p>
            Running costs differ by fuel. A gas rider needs fuel, oil, filters, spark plugs and blades each season, plus fuel stabilizer for winter storage. A battery rider needs only blades, belts and tires, but its batteries will eventually need replacing. Over five to ten years the totals can end up closer than the sticker prices suggest.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whatever you choose, regular maintenance protects the investment. Our <a href="/riding-lawn-mower-maintenance/">riding mower maintenance schedule</a> lists what to check by engine hours and season, and keeping blades sharp makes every mow faster and cleaner on a big lawn.
          </p>

          <p>
            Resale value is the last cost to weigh. Well-known brands with strong dealer networks tend to hold their value better on the used market, which softens the real cost of a premium machine if you sell or trade it in after several seasons. Keep the receipts and service records, because a documented maintenance history helps a used mower sell faster and for more.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="buying-checklist" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. A Buying Checklist for Large Yards
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start with an honest measurement of your mowing area, not your lot size. Subtract the house, driveway, beds and woods. Many two-acre lots have closer to an acre and a half of grass, which changes the right deck size and can bring a cheaper mower into range.
          </p>

          <p>
            Next, walk the lawn and note the slopes, the number of trees and beds, and the narrowest gate or path. Steep spots favor a tractor or walk-behind, many obstacles favor a zero-turn, and a narrow gate caps your deck width. Write the numbers down before you shop.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then decide what else the mower must do. If you tow carts, spread fertilizer or sweep leaves, a lawn or garden tractor is the more useful machine. If mowing is the only job, a zero-turn gives you the fastest results on flat ground. If you want to stop handling gas and oil, check whether a battery rider's rated acreage comfortably exceeds yours.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, compare warranties and dealer support. A no-hour-limit warranty, like the 3-year coverage Cub Cadet lists on the GX54, suits big lawns where hours accumulate quickly. A local dealer that services your brand is worth more than a small price difference online. Our guide to <a href="/what-size-riding-mower-do-i-need/">what size riding mower you need</a> works through the deck math step by step.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sizing to Lot Size Instead of Grass</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A two-acre lot often has only an acre and a half of grass once you remove the house, driveway, beds and woods. Measure the area you actually mow, then size the deck to that, or you may overspend on a mower bigger than you need.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a Zero-Turn for a Hilly Lawn</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Zero-turns steer by braking the rear wheels and have non-steering front casters, so they lose control more easily on side slopes. If your lawn has real hills, choose a lawn tractor or walk-behind and follow the slope limits in the manual.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting Battery Acreage Ratings Blindly</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Battery mower acreage ratings assume dry, regularly mowed grass. Thick spring growth or wet grass can cut runtime substantially. Choose a battery rider rated well above your mowing area, or plan to recharge mid-job.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting the Gate and Storage</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A wide deck that will not fit through your gate or into your shed is a costly mistake. Measure the narrowest access point and your storage space before choosing deck width, and allow for the discharge chute.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping Blade Sharpening</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                On a big lawn, dull blades tear grass, slow you down and strain the engine or batteries. Sharpen blades every 20 to 25 hours of mowing, or more often in sandy soil, and keep a spare set ready.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

