          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best lawn mower for a large yard?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For one to three mostly flat acres, our top pick is the Toro TimeCutter 50 MyRIDE zero-turn. For slopes or towing, the John Deere X380 lawn tractor is better, and for three to four acres, the Cub Cadet XT2 GX54 garden tractor or EGO Z6 52 battery zero-turn.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best mower for 1 acre?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For one acre, a 30-inch walk-behind like the Toro TimeMaster works if you do not mind walking, and a 42-inch lawn tractor or zero-turn is quicker. Slopes favor the walk-behind or a tractor, while many trees and beds favor a zero-turn.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best mower for 2 acres?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For two acres, choose a deck of about 46 to 50 inches. The Toro TimeCutter 50 suits flat lawns with obstacles, the John Deere X380 suits slopes and towing, and the Ryobi 80V 46-inch battery tractor is rated by Ryobi for up to 2.5 acres per charge.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a zero-turn or lawn tractor better for a large yard?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A zero-turn is faster on flat lawns with many trees and beds. A lawn tractor is better on slopes and more useful for towing carts and spreaders. Many large-yard owners with mixed terrain end up preferring a tractor.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a battery mower handle a large yard?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, larger battery riders now can. EGO rates the Z6 52 for up to 4 acres per charge with six batteries and Ryobi rates its 46-inch tractor for up to 2.5 acres. Runtime drops in tall or wet grass, so choose one rated above your acreage.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does it take to mow 2 acres?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on deck width, layout and speed, but a 42-inch rider typically takes about an hour and a half to two hours, while a 50- to 54-inch zero-turn can cut that to around an hour on an open lawn. Obstacles and slopes add time.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a walk-behind mower enough for half an acre?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually, yes. A good 21-inch self-propelled mower handles half an acre in under an hour, and a 30-inch wide walk-behind like the Toro TimeMaster handles up to about an acre while costing far less than a riding mower.
              </p>
            </div>

